ace Solid or Safe Democratic; markets price Lamont at 96-97%", "Only the co/efficient poll (+7) shows a competitive race, with no corroborating evidence"], "rationale": "Every recent poll except one shows Lamont ahead by double digits, all six raters place the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and prediction markets price his win probability around 96-97 percent. With incumbency and a Democratic-leaning state, Fazio would need a large, late shift to close the gap.", "watch": "The Oct. 22 debate and cost-of-living attacks could tighten the race if Fazio gains traction, as the lone +7 poll hints is possible.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Ned Lamont (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Connecticut's governor's race pits incumbent Democrat Ned Lamont against Republican Ryan Fazio. Lamont enters the fall as a clear favorite. Polling since midsummer has shown him ahead by double digits, including a Quinnipiac survey in mid-September with a 17-point lead and a University of New Hampshire poll at 55-38. One recent poll, from co/efficient, showed a narrower seven-point race, but most surveys cluster in the mid-teens. Six expert ratings put the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column.\n\nLamont has gone on air early against Fazio and leads on handling of the cost of living and the economy in recent polling. Prediction markets price his chances around 96-97 percent. Fazio's path would require a major shift in the campaign's final weeks, starting with the first scheduled debate in late October.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.994, "margin": 15.28}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9913, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9896, "margin": 13.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9893, "margin": 14.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9879, "margin": 14.0}]}